# Python conventions (this repo)

- **Line length**: **120** columns maximum for Python (`pyproject.toml` / `ruff format`; `.editorconfig` for editors). Run `ruff format .` from a venv when touching style. Wrap before exceeding: break long strings with **implicit f-string concatenation** (adjacent `f"..."` on consecutive lines), split `assert` / `logger` messages the same way, and break long expressions after commas or inside parentheses.
- **Conditional expression layout**: For `target = a if cond else b`, keep **`a if cond else (` on one line** when `a` and `cond` are short; put only the **longer operand** (usually the `else` call) in parentheses and continue on the next line, closing with `)`. Avoid stacking `if` / `else` on separate lines unless both operands are large—**prefer 2–3 lines over 6–7** when one side is a short literal.
- **Layout**: Package code lives under `hanabi/` (`core`, `console`, `gui`, `ai`, `tools`). Tests under `test/` mirror that structure; use `unittest` and import from `hanabi...` (see `test/core/test_game_engine.py`).
- **Modules**: Start with a short module docstring. Larger modules may use `from __future__ import annotations`, `typing` imports, and `logging.getLogger(__name__)` where already established.
- **Types**: Use `typing` names consistent with nearby files (`List`, `Dict`, `Optional`, etc.). Use `TYPE_CHECKING` for import cycles when the codebase already does.
- **Classes**: Prefer private fields with a leading underscore and `@property` accessors where that pattern exists. Docstrings on public classes and non-obvious methods; avoid verbose comments on obvious code.
- **Attribute access vs local aliases**: Prefer **`self.attr`** (and **`obj.attr`** on parameters) at the point of use so every read shows where the value comes from. Avoid `x = self.attr` unless (1) you need a **fixed snapshot** after `self` will move on (e.g. state before a transition—use a name like `state_before_move` and a one-line comment), (2) the path is **several dots deep** and repeating it hurts clarity within one expression, or (3) a **non-idempotent** call must run once (`x = self.expensive()`). Plain `x = self.common_view` only to shorten lines is discouraged: it hides provenance and can hold a **stale reference** if later code updates `self` while the local still points at the old object.
- **Two-source branch**: If a block must use **either** `self._frozen_state` **or** `self._game.state` (same type, one logical “view”), you may assign once after `if`/`else`—use an explicit name like `paint_state` and a short comment. Prefer **duplicating** `self._frozen_state.foo` / `self._game.state.foo` in the two branches when only one or two attributes are needed.
- **Before/after `self` updates**: When `self._game.state` (or similar) changes mid-method, keep clearly named locals such as `state_before_move`, `old_state` / `new_state`, with a one-line comment—do not rely on a stale binding.
- **Public API naming**: Use **snake_case** for properties, methods, and module-level names on the game layer (`Game`, `GameState`, `PlayerView`, observers, etc.). Class names stay PascalCase.
- **Tests**: Subclass `unittest.TestCase`, use `setUp` for fixtures, and one-line docstrings on test methods describing behavior under test.

# Comparisons

- **Constant on the left** (`==` / `!=`): When comparing to a **literal** (number, string, `True`/`False`) or a **short named constant** (module constant, `Enum` member), put that value **first**—e.g. `4 == hand_size`, `"play" == command`, `NUM_PLAYERS == settings.num_players`, `CardKind.PLAYABLE == kind`. Readers see the expected value immediately; the right-hand side is usually longer. **Do not** apply to idioms that the community standard keeps as-is (e.g. `if __name__ == "__main__":`). Prefer **`x is None`** / **`x is not None`** (identity), not `None == x`.

# Control flow and dispatch

- **Short branches first**: When order is semantically equivalent, handle the **smallest** branch first (fewer lines or simpler predicate) so the main case stays easy to scan.
- **Positive tests when symmetric**: If two branches are about the same size, prefer the branch with the **simpler or positive** condition first (e.g. `x == y` before a multi-line `else`, rather than `x != y` with a one-line `else`) so readers see the special case first and the main case under `else`.
- **Conditional expression** (`x if cond else y`): When both branches only set the **same** variable to a value, use a conditional expression instead of duplicating the assignment in `if`/`else`. Prefer **not** nesting ternaries; use a full `if`/`else` when a branch needs multiple statements or non-trivial side effects.
- **Early exit**: Prefer **guard clauses** (`return` soon) over deep nesting; keep the “happy path” or largest case at the end of the function when that reads best.
- **Exhaustive dispatch** (closed set of variants, e.g. all `Move` subclasses the engine knows): List **every** case explicitly; use `assert False, f"unexpected … {type(x)}"` instead of a silent `else: return` / generic fallback. This matches repo rules: unexpected types are bugs.
- **Event handlers** (`observe`, etc.) where **ignoring** most events is correct: use early handling + `return` for the cases you care about; a final **plain `return`** (no assert) is OK when “everything else is intentionally ignored”—add a one-line comment if non-obvious.
